# Every fusion, beside whether its layer must move with another. One row per fusion, `violates`
# where the layer sits in a co-movement class of more than one.
#
# The fourth falsifier, as a rule
#   The rule is `pm:Layer`'s own definition, which is also the model's fourth falsifier: *a
#   layer is a place where a remainder can be held independently of every other layer*, and *if
#   two candidate layers' remainders always move together, they were never two layers*. If A is
#   composed from B while B is composed from A, no perturbation of one leaves the other fixed.
#   The pair fails the definition: the two cells are one cell.
#
# The repair is a merge, not a deleted edge
#   The detail says so. A rule that reports *break the loop* points at falsifying a real supply
#   relation. What the schema asks for is merging the layers, or one filer withdrawing a part,
#   and that is what the detail names.
#
# It sees what no other rule here sees
#   A local loop is also caught by a rule confined to one document, though at the root grain and
#   pointing at the wrong repair. A cross-document loop whose factors close round it is reached
#   by no local rule at all, and `conversion_cycle_does_not_close` correctly ignores it because
#   the units do close. That case is two layers co-moving with no arithmetic error anywhere, and
#   nothing else here reaches it.
#
# The population is the fusions, exactly
#   Not a convenience: to sit on a cycle a layer must have an outgoing edge in `F`, which is to
#   say it must be composed from something.
#
# One row per fusion, not one per pair
#   The pair relation holds `(A,A)` and `(A,B)` for a two-cycle, so joining it directly reports
#   one defect twice. A rule's grain is its subject's grain, and the subject here is the layer.
#
# The reflexive pair is the simplest violation
#   It is also the easiest to exclude by accident. Write `AND NOT (co_moves_with = self)` so the
#   detail does not read *`compute` moves with `compute`*, and the rule fires zero on a layer
#   composed from itself, which is the purest case of a thing that is not a layer.
#   `composition/descent.sqlc` emits `(A, A)` only when A genuinely reaches A, so a reflexive
#   pair here is never trivial. A cosmetic choice in a detail string makes a hole at the easiest
#   case, so the detail branches on it instead.
#
# Probe
#   Inside a transaction you roll back, close a loop within one document, then a cross-document
#   loop whose factors close round it. This rule must fire on both, and
#   `conversion_cycle_does_not_close` must stay quiet on the second.
-- composition/descent.sqlc intersected with its converse; conformance rule "layers that always move together are one layer".
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT f.filing, f.layer,
           c.filing IS NOT NULL AS violates,
           CASE WHEN c.filing IS NULL
                THEN format('`%s` holds its remainder independently', f.layer)
                WHEN c.partner IS NULL
                THEN format('`%s` is composed from ITSELF, so its figure depends on its own value and no rank exists for it', f.layer)
                ELSE format('`%s` moves with `%s`, and %s layers here were one layer: the repair is to merge them, not to break a part',
                            f.layer, c.partner, c.members)
           END AS detail
    FROM      (
        :compose(composition/fusions.sqlc)
    ) f
    LEFT JOIN (
        SELECT m.filing, m.layer, m.class, m.members,
               bool_or(m.co_moves_with_filing = m.filing AND m.co_moves_with_layer = m.layer)
                 AS reaches_itself,
               min(m.co_moves_with_filing || '/' || m.co_moves_with_layer)
                 FILTER (WHERE NOT (m.co_moves_with_filing = m.filing
                                AND m.co_moves_with_layer = m.layer)) AS partner
        FROM (
            :compose(rank/co_moving_layers.sqlc)
        ) m
        GROUP BY m.filing, m.layer, m.class, m.members
    ) c ON c.filing = f.filing AND c.layer = f.layer
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'layers_move_together'
